WEBVTT

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:10.894
When you prepare a notice, plan a meeting or sit down with a family,
the history a community carries is often in the room with you,

00:00:10.894 --> 00:00:12.878
whether or not anyone names it.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.811
The Minnesota Communities pages exist to help you notice that
history and respond to it well.

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.167
Read the short introduction at the top.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:29.950
It says these briefs offer context so you can ask better questions
and plan for access, never to describe any one person.

00:00:30.072 --> 00:00:33.306
That last phrase is the heart of how to use them.

00:00:33.872 --> 00:00:39.046
Open a brief, such as Hmong Minnesota, and the Community brief view
opens first.

00:00:39.168 --> 00:00:45.690
Before any history, the Community overview reminds you how much
variety lives inside one community.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:53.425
In this brief, it notes that elders and young people born here may
differ sharply on language and family authority, and that clan

00:00:53.425 --> 00:00:56.380
matters for some families and not for others.

00:00:56.502 --> 00:01:04.427
Just below, Names and terms explains that given names may change at
marriage, and it ends with a plain instruction: ask how the person

00:01:04.427 --> 00:01:06.028
wants to be addressed.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.241
Every brief starts by widening your view rather than narrowing it.

00:01:10.808 --> 00:01:17.298
The section called History, culture, and life today holds longer
readings you can open one at a time.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:25.251
Inside People, place, and perspective, a short passage titled How to
read this explains how the whole brief is meant to work.

00:01:25.373 --> 00:01:33.588
It calls the page an orientation, and it warns that a stereotype is
what happens when the page becomes the person in the lobby.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:40.872
It asks you to defer to the person in front of you, treating what
you read as a hypothesis you still test with each family.

00:01:40.994 --> 00:01:44.610
That is the difference between preparation and profiling.

00:01:45.177 --> 00:01:49.703
Practice considerations is where context becomes something you can
use.

00:01:49.826 --> 00:01:54.176
Open What to ask, and you will find questions rather than
conclusions.

00:01:54.298 --> 00:02:01.249
For a Hmong household, the brief suggests asking which Hmong to
book, White Hmong or Green Hmong, who should be in the room for this

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:04.814
decision, and how names are written on the person's documents.

00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:11.103
It also says that traditional healers or church leaders should be
involved only when the family names them.

00:02:11.226 --> 00:02:16.148
Each question hands the answer back to the person, which is exactly
where it belongs.

00:02:16.714 --> 00:02:21.281
Access considerations turns the same thinking toward your own
process.

00:02:21.403 --> 00:02:28.612
In the Somali Minnesota brief, the list asks that an interpreter
request name the dialect and any gender-match request, that forms

00:02:28.612 --> 00:02:35.732
accept three-part names without an invented surname, and that a
phone or in-person path exist for people without broadband.

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:39.710
On timing, it says to ask rather than assume observance.

00:02:39.832 --> 00:02:43.970
Notice that every item describes something your team can change.

00:02:44.092 --> 00:02:50.259
The brief points your attention at the door people must walk
through, not at the people themselves.

00:02:50.826 --> 00:02:55.268
Assumptions to examine is the part many people find most useful.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:03.373
The Somali brief cautions you not to assume that a smile, silence or
hospitality means consent to a plan, and not to fold one household

00:03:03.373 --> 00:03:07.102
into another community's brief because of a shared region.

00:03:07.224 --> 00:03:14.448
Nearby, Who to involve reminds you not to expect colleagues who
share a language to interpret without compensation or to speak for

00:03:14.448 --> 00:03:15.859
an entire community.

00:03:15.981 --> 00:03:23.275
When you switch to Learning and reflection, short situations with an
Explore the reasoning button let you practice this thinking

00:03:23.275 --> 00:03:25.170
privately, at your own pace.

00:03:25.737 --> 00:03:30.061
The 11 Tribal Nations page works differently, and on purpose.

00:03:30.183 --> 00:03:37.884
A notice at the top explains that this program defers to the Office
of Indian Affairs and to the Department of Human Services offices

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:44.831
that handle Tribal relations and consultation for anything
concerning Native American individuals, communities or Tribal

00:03:44.831 --> 00:03:45.594
Nations.

00:03:45.717 --> 00:03:48.950
That deference is standing, not a brief under construction.

00:03:49.072 --> 00:03:57.024
Each Nation is a sovereign government with its own consultation
process, so for anything substantive, contact those offices

00:03:57.024 --> 00:03:57.906
directly.

00:03:58.028 --> 00:04:02.694
The same note appears on the Alaska Native and Native American
pages.

00:04:03.261 --> 00:04:09.872
Before your next meeting, notice or outreach plan, choose one brief
that connects to the people you serve.

00:04:09.994 --> 00:04:16.418
Read its Practice considerations, pick two questions from What to
ask, and carry them into the conversation.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.447
Then listen closely to the answers.

00:04:19.569 --> 00:04:26.001
Let the person you are working with, not the page, shape what
happens next, and you will be using these briefs exactly as they

00:04:26.001 --> 00:04:27.424
were meant to be used.
